// The revl stdlib MEMORY module (issue #2696).
//
// A typed memory service for a long-lived, model-driven component: the notes a
// component keeps between turns, with the origin and the retention of a stored
// item carried by the item and not forgotten at the store boundary.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------ the need
// A memory item is whatever store a provider offers, typed as a plain value.
// The pieces that make it safe exist separately: `Untrusted[T]`, `Retained[T, P]`,
// realm placement, and the refusal of a `Secret` flowing to a prompt. This
// module ties them to a stored item. It is a LIBRARY, not a new language
// construct: it adds no syntax and no checker rule.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the surface
// `put`, `get` and `forget` are the three kv-scoped crossings that write, read
// and drop one item. `put` and `get` are generic over the item's base type:
//
//     use "stdlib/memory.rvl" { put, get, forget, MemoryMeta }
//
//     let ok = emit put("note-1", note, { origin: "web", purpose: "draft", policy: "" })
//     let back: Opt[Str] = emit get("note-1")
//     emit forget("note-1")
//
// A value put as `Untrusted[Str]` comes back untrusted: the read carries the
// origin the write left on the resource, so a `Trusted[T]` sink still refuses
// it exactly as it refused the value before it was stored. The generic type of
// `get` is the caller's choice of the base type; the QUALIFIER is not a type
// argument and cannot be chosen away, because it is not a type-level thing at
// the call. It is the taint the resource fixpoint carries, and it survives the
// store boundary regardless of how the read is typed.
//
// --------------------------------------------------- why the write is a receiver
// `put` declares its value `Secret[T]`. That is the RECEIVER half of the
// `Secret[T]` rule (docs/design/256-capability-bound-secrets.md §7b): a
// not-for-prompt item typed `Secret[T]` may be written, and the declaration is
// admission-only, so a plain item is admitted unchanged and is NOT made
// confidential by the receiver. The receiver is what lets a `Secret[T]` item be
// stored at all; the READ is where its confidence is restored, by the same
// resource fixpoint that restores every other origin, and an undeclared
// receiver (a log, an ordinary serialization, an LLM prompt) then refuses it.
//
// A consequence worth stating: the declared receiver also stamps the value as a
// `Secret` parameter on the crossing, so the runtime's audit recorder redacts
// the written argument in its trace. That is conservative in the safe
// direction: a memory write is exactly where a confidential item may land, and
// a plain item's trace is redacted too rather than risk the other way.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------ the #1983 fixpoint
// The WRITE and the READ name the SAME declared persistence scope (`kv`), so
// they are siblings of one resource. The compiler already models a persistence
// resource as flow-insensitive shared state: whatever entered it at any crossing
// is reachable by any later read of it, in this body, in another body, or across
// the call graph. `get` is such a read, so it mints what `put` recorded. Nothing
// in this file re-derives that rule; both crossings name the `kv` scope and the
// existing `_mint_reload` does the rest. This is also why `put` and `get` must
// stay a PAIR on one scope: splitting them across scopes would make the read
// clean and the store would launder the origin it exists to keep.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------ metadata is data
// `MemoryMeta` is a plain typed record: the origin label, the purpose and the
// retention policy NAME. The fields are INFORMATIONAL. The checker does not
// read them and they enforce nothing: `meta.policy` is a string, not a
// `Retained[T, P]` declaration, and `meta.purpose` is a label. What is enforced
// lives on the value: an item is untrusted, confidential or retained because the
// value carries that qualifier. Keep the fields accurate for the audit trail,
// and rely on the types for the refusal. In particular a plain value whose
// `meta.policy` names an expired policy is NOT refused, because the field is
// not the qualifier; a `Retained[T, P]` value is refused at `put` by the same
// G-RETAIN every other persistence sink raises.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------- what is not covered
// A value COMPUTED from a stored item (an embedding, a summary) is a
// DERIVATIVE, and derivative handling is the separate design question tracked
// by issue #2695. This module stores and returns an item unchanged; it does not
// invent a second rule for derivatives.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------- tier status
// The three crossings carry a py-tier reference store (an in-process map) so
// the module is runnable and testable today. A real deployment backs the same
// three crossing NAMES with its own host store: the types, the taint and the
// retention enforcement live above the crossing and do not change with the
// backend. Other tiers are a follow-up, exactly as `stdlib/net.rvl`'s py-tier
// crossing is.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------ bump discipline
// Adding, removing or changing a `pub` symbol here bumps `stdlib/version.rvl`
// (see its bump log).

// The item's informational record. Every field is a plain `Str`: the label of
// the source the item came from, the purpose the caller keeps it for, and the
// retention policy NAME (empty when none applies). See "metadata is data"
// above: the checker reads none of these.
pub type MemoryMeta = {
  origin: Str,
  purpose: Str,
  policy: Str,
}

// WRITE one item into the `kv` resource. The value is declared `Secret[T]` so a
// not-for-prompt (`Secret[T]`) item is admitted at this crossing; the receiver
// declaration is admission-only, so a plain value is not made confidential by
// it. `meta` is stored beside the value and is never consulted by the checker.
pub extern emission[kv.memory] fn put[T](key: Str, value: Secret[T],
                                         meta: MemoryMeta) -> Bool
  = @py {
    global _revl_memory_items
    try:
        items = _revl_memory_items
    except NameError:
        items = {}
        _revl_memory_items = items
    items[key] = {"value": value, "meta": meta}
    return True
  }

// READ one item, or `None` when the key is absent. The value comes back with the
// origins the resource fixpoint recorded for the `kv` scope, so the qualifier it
// was written with is the qualifier it is read with.
pub extern emission[kv.memory] fn get[T](key: Str) -> Opt[T]
  = @py {
    items = globals().get("_revl_memory_items") or {}
    entry = items.get(key)
    if entry is None:
        return None
    return entry["value"]
  }

// DROP one item. Forgetting is a write to the same `kv` resource. It returns
// `Unit` on purpose: the read half's mint would otherwise attach the stored
// origins to a returned `Bool`, and a success flag is not item data.
pub extern emission[kv.memory] fn forget(key: Str) -> Unit
  = @py {
    items = globals().get("_revl_memory_items") or {}
    items.pop(key, None)
    return
  }
